1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
Harbor Learning Circle has three neat classroom briefs on a table.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,600
All three were produced on time.

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:07,460
Morgan looks past the count.

4
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:11,000
One brief treats an unconfirmed facilitator as confirmed.

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,380
Another stopped because the note was stale.

6
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:17,200
The third accurately says the facilitator is still under review.

7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,500
Three drafts tell Morgan very little about whether this work helps the team.

8
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:25,040
Last lesson gave us a contract and three test cases.

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:30,860
This lesson asks what we would watch over time, and what Morgan must see before signing a decision.

10
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:40,240
Chapter Fifteen lays out seven rungs: activity, output, quality, behavior, outcome, learning, and authority.

11
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,660
A count of briefs belongs low on the ladder.

12
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:48,100
For Harbor, quality asks whether each sentence is supported by a permitted, current source.

13
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:53,660
Behavior asks whether the preparer stopped at a stale note and respected the prepare-only gate.

14
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:59,160
Outcome asks whether Morgan could decide with less reconstruction and no hidden staffing claim.

15
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:04,700
Learning asks whether a revised contract reduces the same error in later approved tests.

16
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:09,300
Authority records the human-granted scope; it is not a prize for a high count.

17
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:12,560
The workbook adds cost and trust to the scorecard.

18
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:19,800
Harbor can record course minutes for Morgan's review and an illustrative preparation cost, but cannot claim real savings.

19
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:28,380
For the paper case, the useful measures are source-supported claims, correct holds, Morgan's review burden, and whether a decision becomes clearer.

20
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:30,920
Write a decision beside each measure.

21
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:37,620
If a measure cannot tell Morgan to keep, revise, narrow, or stop the design, take it off the front page.

22
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,220
Three classroom cases are a test exercise, not a performance baseline.

23
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:46,820
Chapter Sixteen asks us to choose a review posture for each action.

24
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,560
Pre-action review means the work waits for a human before a side effect.

25
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:57,040
Bounded action means a specifically fenced action may happen inside an already approved scope.

26
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:02,200
Post-action audit checks low-stakes action after it happens, with a way to tighten the fence.

27
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,320
In this course, these are design choices on paper.

28
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:14,300
Harbor's brief may be prepared for Morgan; proposing a session, announcing it, booking a room, or spending would need its own explicit human gate.

29
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,220
Nothing in our evaluation grants those permissions.

30
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:27,120
Give Morgan a review view that answers four questions: what changed, which sources support it, what is uncertain, and what happens if Morgan says yes.

31
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:30,980
Make reject and request-revision as visible as approve.

32
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:36,160
The stale facilitator note should land as a hold with a next step, not a green badge.

33
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:40,020
A reviewer who sees only a score will learn to clear a queue.

34
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,960
A reviewer who sees the evidence can take responsibility for a decision.

35
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:45,080
Pause.

36
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,760
Which number would make Harbor look busy while hiding the unsupported facilitator claim?

37
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:54,160
Add an Agent Metrics Ladder and Review Postures card to your Operating File.

38
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:59,920
Name one measure for activity, quality, behavior or trust, cost, and outcome.

39
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:05,700
Give each a source, a review cadence, a named decider, and the decision it could change.

40
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:08,740
Mark any unavailable baseline as unproven.

41
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:16,420
Then classify three Harbor actions: drafting the brief, Morgan's internal review, and a proposed external announcement.

42
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:19,840
For each, state the reviewer view and stop condition.

43
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,140
Use a fictional or approved example.

44
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:27,480
Do not use private operational data or paste live dashboards or customer results.

45
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:14,820
Save revision nine as a draft.

46
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:17,020
The scorecard may tell Morgan where to look.

47
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:18,860
It does not sign for Morgan.

48
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:23,360
Next we will put the same discipline around money and the final decision brief.
